Schema drift check: docs/manifest-format.md vs tools/nova_trust.py
================================================================

Date: 2026-10-02
Adds: tests/test_schema_drift.py, tests/fixtures/manifest-schema.json,
       one line in scripts/check.sh stage 4/13
Base commit: 344344a "C2: make top-level bootchain optional again, as the spec says"

What it is for
--------------
docs/manifest-format.md opens with: "If this file and the code disagree, one
of them has a bug." That sentence was unenforced. df69025 changed the code's
top-level required set and left the doc untouched; 15 of 24 tests went red and
sat red, and the doc/code disagreement itself had no test at all. This check is
the missing test.

Three artefacts, no two derived from each other:
  1. docs/manifest-format.md            the specification, written prose
  2. tests/fixtures/manifest-schema.json transcribed from that prose by hand
  3. tools/nova_trust.py                the implementation
Two edges are checked separately: doc -> fixture by parsing the doc's markdown
field table and its bootchain JSON example; fixture -> code by probing.

Why the code side is probed rather than diffed
----------------------------------------------
The obvious implementation is to import nova_trust's _TOP / _RELEASE / _TRUST /
_PACKAGE / _BOOTCHAIN* constants and diff them against the fixture. That is
void under AGENTS.md rule 1: those constants and structure_errors() are the same
file, written by the same author, so the comparison could not fail even if the
doc were rewritten to say the opposite. tests/test_schema_drift.py instead
deletes one field at a time from a manifest that structure_errors() accepts with
zero errors, and asks whether it complained. A field the code rejects when
absent is required; one it tolerates is optional. That measures behaviour, so it
can fail. The transcript in section 3 is that proof.

The drift this found
--------------------
Real, and it predates this commit. docs/manifest-format.md:139, under
"Rules (when present)":

    - Both `limine` and `kernel` are required.

tools/nova_trust.py read instead:

    for comp_name in _BOOTCHAIN:
        if comp_name not in bc:
            continue  # optional, but if present must be valid

so a manifest whose bootchain section pinned only limine was accepted. The
signature then covered a boot chain with an unpinned kernel. Section 2 below is
the transcript of the check failing on exactly this before the code was fixed.
df69025 is the later commit on this branch (git rev-list --count df69025..d8cda96
= 0, so df69025 is the descendant), and it did not update the doc: the doc is
the standing spec, the code is what drifted. Per AGENTS.md rule 2 the spec wins,
so nova_trust.py was corrected to the doc, not the doc to the code. The test was
not edited to pass (rule 7); its assertions are exactly as first written.

