2. First run of the new check: 14 failures, 3 of them real
-------------------------------------------------------
$ py -3 tests/test_schema_drift.py 2>&1 | tail -3
Ran 11 tests in 0.015s

FAILED (failures=14)

Fourteen, not two. Eleven of those were bugs in the check I had just written,
and they are recorded here because "the new test found a real drift" is only
worth anything if the test itself was honest first:

  - where_level("bootchain.limine") walked m["limine"] instead of
    m["bootchain"]["limine"], so it returned a throwaway {} and every
    bootchain.limine / bootchain.kernel mutation went nowhere. 9 of the 14.
  - the doc field table has no bare `release` or `trust` row - it lists
    release.version, trust.algorithm and so on - so top-level names must be
    inferred from the dotted prefixes rather than read off the rows.
  - packages[i].id yields "packages[i]", and [i] is an element selector, not
    a field name.

Fixed in the check, not in its assertions. Re-run:

