8. What this check does NOT cover
---------------------------------
Shape only: which fields exist, which are required, which are optional, and
whether unlisted fields are errors. It does not check the doc's prose rules
(regexes, >= 64 hex, no wildcards, sequence == i+1). Those are value rules and
remain tests/test_trust.py's job; two oracles that both tried to cover
everything would be two places to be wrong.

It also cannot tell you the doc is *correct*, only that the code matches it. If
the doc and the code are both wrong the same way, this check is green and the
error is elsewhere. That is what the external oracles in stages 7 and 9 are
for.

9. Full 13-stage check: stages 5-13 fail on this host, not on this change
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
scripts/check.sh was run end to end and reported FAILURES. Details, because
the log is committed and a reader will find it:

  stage  4/13 host tests                 PASS (24 + 11 tests, above)
  stage  5/13 structural verification    FAIL, 2 checks
  stage  6/13 fuzz the verifier          BASELINE FAIL (cascades from 5)
  stage  7/13 external oracles           0 failed, 3 SKIPPED (no sgdisk, no
                                         fsck.fat, no mdir on this host)
  stage  8/13 QEMU boot tests            4x FAIL, all exit=124
  stage  9/13 FAT32 external oracles     determinism ok, 2 SKIPPED
  stage 10/13 CMOS RTC bracket oracle    FAIL, rc=124
  stage 11/13 capability oracle          manifest half PASS (verify-manifest:
                                         PASS), guest half FAIL, rc=124
  stage 12/13 interactive shell oracle   FAIL, no `nova> ` prompt
  stage 13/13 ELF64 loader oracle         fixture + host oracle PASS, guest
                                         half FAIL, rc=124

Root cause, and it is not this change. scripts/build-disk.sh:123 runs

    "$TOOL" bios-install "$OUT"

and the full run logged, twice:

    .../limine-tool-windows-x86/limine.exe: Permission denied

so the BIOS boot stages are never written into build/nova.hdd. Stage 5 then
reports "MBR stage-1 boot code present (bios-install ran)" as FAIL, and QEMU
boots to "Booting from Hard Disk..." and produces nothing further:

$ timeout 40 "/c/Program Files/qemu/qemu-system-x86_64.exe" -drive file=build/nova.hdd,format=raw,if=ide -m 256 -nographic -serial file:build/manual-serial.log -display none >build/manual-out.log 2>&1; echo "QEMU_RC=$?"
QEMU_RC=124
-- serial bytes: 315 --
SeaBIOS (version rel-1.17.0-0-gb52ca86e094d-prebuilt.qemu.org)
...
Booting from Hard Disk...

Isolated to the bare binary, with no project code in the invocation:

$ "$TOOL" bios-install build/scratch.hdd; echo "LIMINE_RC=$?"
LIMINE_RC=126
/usr/bin/bash: line 1: .../limine.exe: Permission denied

And the disk path does not read the code this change touched:

$ grep -n 'nova_trust\|manifest' scripts/build-disk.sh
58:  # manifest hints printed below (the manifest schema is sha256-based).
129:  # tools/manifest/bootchain.sha256, so the compiled tool inherits the
145:# C4: Also compute and display Limine hash for the manifest
151:  echo "build-disk: bootchain hashes (for manifest when signing release):"
154:  echo "build-disk: when ready to sign, pass these to create-manifest.py:"

Comments and echo text only. Every stage that fails from 5 onward needs a BIOS-
bootable image; stages 10-13 and 12 all report rc=124, the same timeout, all
gated on the guest actually booting.

This is NOT the failure documented in docs/sac-build-blocks.md: that one is
rust-lld failing with os error 4551 / CodeIntegrity events. This is a plain
exec denial, rc=126, of a different binary. Same family of host enforcement,
most likely, but I did not check the Windows event log and cannot claim the
cause. It is unverified and is recorded as such.

Note also that docs/logs/k4b-check-full.log (commit 65e0270) has stages 5-13
green on this same path, so this block appeared on this host between that
commit and now. Fixing it is outside this work order: it needs either an
unblocked limine.exe or a different way to install the BIOS stages, and that is
not a call this commit gets to make on its own.

Therefore: stage 4, the stage this change touches, is green; the full check is
red for a reason recorded above and not caused by this change. Stating that
plainly is more useful than a green line that is not true.